        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An anoscope is a short, hollow instrument used to examine the anal canal and the surrounding distal anorectal region. It allows direct visualisation of the anal mucosa and is commonly used in general surgery, colorectal surgery and proctology."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is particularly useful for investigating anal bleeding, suspected haemorrhoids, anal fissures, anal canal lesions and selected inflammatory conditions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key OSCE identification point: A short, rigid, hollow tube, usually supplied with an obturator, that permits direct examination of the anal canal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unlike a sigmoidoscope, an anoscope is designed primarily for the anal canal rather than examination of the sigmoid colon."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Classification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Specialty: General surgery and colorectal surgery.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Function: Diagnostic examination and procedural access.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Design: Usually short and rigid.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Material: Stainless steel or medical-grade plastic.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Viewing method: Direct visualisation, with illumination where required.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Accessories: Obturator, light source or integrated illumination, and compatible procedural attachments in selected models.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/anoscope_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Description and function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Barrel or tube",
                      "Holds the anal canal open for visualisation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Distal tip",
                      "Inserted end, designed to be smooth and atraumatic."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Obturator",
                      "Fills the barrel during insertion to provide a smoother leading surface."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Handle or grip",
                      "Allows controlled manipulation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Viewing aperture",
                      "Provides a view of the mucosa and, depending on the design, access for instruments."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Light source or illumination attachment",
                      "Illuminates the anal canal."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Instrument port, where present",
                      "Permits introduction of compatible instruments for selected procedures."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Not every anoscope has an integrated light source or an instrument port. Label the components present on the actual instrument."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The anoscope works by gently separating the tissues of the anal canal and providing a stable viewing passage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The instrument and obturator are inspected.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The patient is positioned appropriately and the perianal region examined.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The lubricated anoscope is introduced gently, usually with the obturator in place.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The obturator is removed once the instrument is positioned appropriately.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The anal mucosa is inspected under adequate illumination.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The instrument is withdrawn carefully.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Anoscopy is a direct examination technique. It does not ordinarily require advancement into the sigmoid colon."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Types of anoscope",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/anoscope_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Reusable metal anoscope"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Usually made of medical-grade stainless steel.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Durable and suitable for repeated use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires validated cleaning and sterilisation.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/anoscope_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Disposable plastic anoscope"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Designed for single-patient use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May permit visualisation through a transparent barrel.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Must be discarded after use according to clinical waste policy.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/anoscope_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Illuminated anoscope"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Incorporates or connects to a light source.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improves visualisation of the anal mucosa.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Design and attachment vary by manufacturer.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Indications and clinical uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Indication",
                    "What it helps assess"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Rectal bleeding",
                      "Anal fissures, haemorrhoids and other visible anal canal lesions."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Suspected haemorrhoids",
                      "Internal haemorrhoidal cushions and associated bleeding."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Anal pain",
                      "Selected mucosal lesions and possible sources of discomfort."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Anal fissure",
                      "Location and appearance, when examination is tolerable."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Anal canal lesions",
                      "Ulcers, polyps, masses or suspicious mucosal changes."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Proctitis or inflammation",
                      "Visible inflammatory changes in the examined area."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Biopsy",
                      "Selected lesions, using compatible instruments and appropriate technique."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Selected office procedures",
                      "May facilitate procedures such as rubber-band ligation of internal haemorrhoids when indicated and performed by trained personnel."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Anoscopy is often helpful when bleeding is thought to arise from the anal canal. However, finding haemorrhoids does not exclude a more proximal source of bleeding."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Quick examination of the anal canal.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Direct visualisation of internal haemorrhoids and other mucosal lesions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Often performed without sedation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Relatively simple equipment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can facilitate selected diagnostic and therapeutic procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires less extensive preparation than a full colonoscopy in many circumstances.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Examines only a limited anatomical region.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May cause discomfort or pain, especially with fissures or acute inflammation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Does not evaluate the entire rectum or colon.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Stool or blood can obscure the field.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires adequate illumination and appropriate technique.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Some lesions require further examination, biopsy or endoscopy.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use clinical judgement and seek specialist assessment when examination may cause harm."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Important situations requiring caution include:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Severe anal pain or an acute fissure that prevents tolerable examination.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Marked anal stenosis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Suspected anorectal injury or perforation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Severe acute inflammation or a painful anorectal abscess.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Haemodynamic instability or major active bleeding requiring resuscitation first.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Recent anorectal surgery, depending on the procedure and healing status.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A painful examination should not be forced. Examination under anaesthesia or an alternative assessment may be appropriate in selected cases."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. OSCE procedure: how to perform anoscopy",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/anoscope_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 1 — Preparation"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Introduce yourself, confirm identity, explain the procedure and obtain consent. Provide privacy, arrange a chaperone according to local policy, perform hand hygiene and prepare gloves, lubricant, light source and anoscope."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/anoscope_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 2 — Positioning"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The left lateral position with hips and knees flexed is commonly used. Alternative positions may be appropriate depending on the clinical setting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 3 — Preliminary examination"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the perianal skin for fissures, external haemorrhoids, ulcers, discharge and swelling. Perform a digital rectal examination when appropriate and tolerated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/anoscope_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 4 — Insertion"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lubricate the instrument and introduce it gently with the obturator in place, following the instrument's design and standard clinical technique. Never force insertion against pain or resistance."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/anoscope_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 5 — Inspection"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Remove the obturator as appropriate, illuminate the canal and inspect the mucosa systematically. Identify haemorrhoids, fissures where visible, ulcers, masses and bleeding. Perform sampling or a procedure only when indicated and within the operator's competence."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/anoscope_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 6 — Completion"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Withdraw the instrument gently, check patient comfort, document the findings and arrange further investigation if needed. Dispose of or reprocess the instrument according to its classification and manufacturer's instructions."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Normal and abnormal findings",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Finding",
                    "Possible significance"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Intact, smooth mucosa",
                      "Normal appearance in the examined area."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Enlarged internal haemorrhoidal cushions",
                      "Internal haemorrhoidal disease, interpreted alongside symptoms and examination."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ulcer or linear tear",
                      "Possible fissure or another ulcerative process."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Erythema and friability",
                      "Inflammation or proctitis."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Polypoid lesion",
                      "Anal canal polyp or another mucosal lesion requiring assessment."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Irregular mass",
                      "Suspicious lesion requiring appropriate diagnostic investigation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Fresh blood",
                      "Active or recent bleeding; source requires assessment."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Poor visibility due to stool or blood",
                      "Limited examination; pathology may be missed."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A normal anoscopy does not exclude disease higher in the rectum or colon."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Prevention"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Pain or discomfort",
                      "Explain the procedure and use gentle technique."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Minor bleeding",
                      "Avoid force and unnecessary manipulation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mucosal injury",
                      "Use adequate lubrication and an appropriate instrument."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Vasovagal reaction",
                      "Monitor symptoms and stop if the patient becomes faint or unwell."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection transmission",
                      "Follow standard precautions and validated reprocessing protocols."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Rare perforation or serious injury",
                      "Avoid forceful insertion, particularly in diseased or vulnerable tissue."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Missed pathology",
                      "Recognise the limited examination range and arrange further assessment when indicated."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. Anoscope vs proctoscope vs sigmoidoscope",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Anoscope",
                    "Proctoscope",
                    "Sigmoidoscope"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Main region",
                      "Anal canal",
                      "Anal canal and distal rectum",
                      "Rectum and sigmoid colon"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Typical design",
                      "Short, rigid tube",
                      "Longer tube; rigid or other designs",
                      "Rigid or flexible"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Common use",
                      "Haemorrhoids and anal lesions",
                      "Distal rectal assessment",
                      "Distal colonic assessment"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Biopsy possible",
                      "With compatible equipment",
                      "With compatible equipment",
                      "Yes, with compatible equipment"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Exam limitation",
                      "Limited anatomical reach",
                      "Limited reach compared with sigmoidoscopy",
                      "Does not examine the entire colon"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "anoscope_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "14. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "anoscope_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify this instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An anoscope, used to examine the anal canal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the function of the obturator?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It fills the barrel during insertion and helps provide a smoother leading surface."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the principal indication for anoscopy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examination of the anal canal, commonly for suspected haemorrhoids, anal bleeding and other anal lesions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Can anoscopy diagnose all causes of rectal bleeding?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It examines a limited region. Further investigation may be necessary according to the clinical presentation and risk factors."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is the difference between internal and external haemorrhoids?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Internal haemorrhoids arise above the dentate line and are covered by mucosa; external haemorrhoids arise below it and are covered by anoderm or skin. Anoscopy is useful for assessing internal haemorrhoids."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Why should a painful anal examination not be forced?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It may worsen pain and cause tissue injury. An alternative approach or examination under anaesthesia may be needed in selected cases."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Name three complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pain, minor bleeding and mucosal injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What is the difference between anoscopy and colonoscopy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Anoscopy examines the anal canal; colonoscopy is designed to examine the colon extensively, usually to the caecum when complete."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
